# Game 7 — Black versus 20k

Result: White wins by 28 points (W+28), after B65 pass and W66 pass. Chinese area, komi 7, simple ko, no suicide. Captures before scoring: Black 5, White 0. Exact record: game-007.sgf. Fifth scored game; no demonstrated win at any rank. All earlier game notes were read. Current-position code listed existing groups and distinct liberties after each opponent move from W4 onward; the initial stones were counted manually. No engine, move-ranking code, Monte Carlo, or future-sequence search program was used. Tactical reading and strategic judgments were manual.

Opening: B E5/G3/G7, W C5/D7/D3. E3/E4 connected the center downward. White F6/F5/G5 pushed into the right framework; Black G6/F4/G4 blocked, retaining outside liberties. White H6/H7/H8 separated an upper Black chain from the lower chain. Black F7/E7/G8/E8 then E9/G9/H9 expanded the upper chain, but D8/D9/J8 restricted it. After W32 J8, nine Black stones had only {F9,J9}, with White F8 in atari at F9. Capturing immediately at F9 would create one eye at F8 while White could restrict the remaining route at J9. The top extensions did not establish independent life. Do not treat them as a sound opening merely because the later capture succeeded.

Useful capture: B35 H5 forced W36 D6 to connect White's central stones leftward, leaving the separate White right chain {H6,H7,H8,J8} with {J6,J7,J9}. B37 J6 reduced it to {J7,J9}, but the new Black stone itself had only {J5,J7}. B39 J5 connected it to H5 and the lower chain; the merged chain had six liberties. This was a checked outside connection, not an assumption that connecting ensures safety. White played on the bottom instead of contesting this fight.

B41 F9 captured White F8, leaving the upper chain {F8,J9}. F8 was now a real enclosed eye: all four neighbors E8/G8/F7/F9 were Black. B43 J7 joined the lower chain and put the four White right stones in atari at J9. White could not legally fill J9: it would fill its own last liberty without capturing Black, whose upper group still had F8. B45 J9 captured all four. B47 J8 then joined the upper and lower Black chains while preserving F8 and the separate vertical eye region H6/H7/H8. This was the first substantial actual capture in the series, but it did not win. This record does not establish that White's earlier replies were forced or that the whole upper group was always rescuable.

Area failure: White E1/F1/F2/G2/D1/H2/J2/H3/J3 took the bottom, connecting to the left chain at D1. Black's right-hand area remained too small. At the finish, the main Black chain had 26 stones plus four enclosed empty points {F8,H6,H7,H8}, or 30 area. If White owned the remaining 51 points, the margin would be 51+7-30 = 28, matching the result. This is a consistency check, not the scorer's explicit dead-stone report.

Upper-left entry: B49 B7 began with four liberties and substantial-looking open space. The proposed upper-corner and lower-left directions were not secured independent eye regions. W50 B6 immediately blocked the lower route. B51 C7 extended into the gap, but W52 C8 blocked the upper-right boundary. B53 B8, W54 A7 left the group {A8,B9,C6}. B55 B9 enlarged the top space while White played J2 elsewhere; its liberties became {A8,A9,C6,C9}. A8/A9 were ONE connected potential eye region. C9 bordered White C8/D9 and was not an eye. C6 likewise bordered White B6/C5/D6. The extra liberty count did not establish life.

B57 B5 tried to prevent B6 from connecting via B5 and create a capture, but started a separate Black group with only {A5,B4}. W58 C6 connected B6 to the main White chain through the other route, leaving the upper-left Black group {A8,A9,C9}, still no two-eye construction. B59 B4 extended the new cutting group toward the lower left, but W60 B3 closed the direct bottom route. It finished with {A4,A5,C4}: A4/A5 were one open region, and C4 bordered White C5/D4. No concrete rescue was identified for either left group. The game did not prove every continuation fails.

Endgame: B61 F3 took the central shared boundary point; W62 H3 answered on the bottom-right boundary. B63 J4, W64 J3 completed that boundary. Black preserved the four internal liberties in two separate regions and did not fill them. B65 pass and W66 pass produced W+28. No dead-stone breakdown was returned.

Lessons for game 8:

- The right-side capture worked because Black's eye at F8 was a private liberty, while White's last liberty J9 was shared with the upper Black group. Count both groups before a capturing race, and check whether filling a last liberty actually captures anything.
- Connect threatened attacking stones outside before relying on them. J5 gave J6 access to the lower chain's liberties and made J7 a safe attacking move.
- A tactical capture and group rescue are insufficient if the resulting area is only 30. Assess board share while the framework is still expanding; opening play repeatedly conceded most of the left and bottom.
- Once B6 removed the lower route and C8 restricted the top-right, the B7 invasion needed a revised concrete plan. Calling the corner broad or naming two directions repeated game 6's mistake.
- Name the actual empty regions and their required boundary points. A8/A9 were adjacent; C9 had enemy neighbors. Four liberties did not mean two usable eyes.
- Before a cut, check ALL enemy connection routes. B5 blocked only one route; C6 joined B6 directly to White's established chain. The new B5 group had no secured eyes or connection.
- Do not turn one failed invasion into another without a life plan. B4's extension was blocked at B3 and reproduced the same narrow-edge failure.
- Manual liberty reasoning can also be mistaken even when the counting code is accurate. During consideration, C6 was briefly treated as an isolated suicide point; it would have joined existing Black C7 while other liberties remained. Check legality for the resulting whole group when uncertain, not just the new stone's immediate empty neighbors.
- Retain the concrete right-side eye preservation, but do not infer improved overall strength from five captures. Seven games, seven losses; remain at 20k until an actual scored win supports moving up. Ultimate goal remains consistent wins against 9d.
